\subsection{Vintages vs latest — the revision trap}{

FRED and ALFRED are the same host, split only by the \code{realtime_start} /
\code{realtime_end} window and the \code{output_type}. The \strong{latest} value of an
observation is simply the value at today's real-time window; it is not a
separate endpoint or code path. \code{get_series()} exposes the window directly:
omit it for the latest values, or pass a past \code{realtime_start} to see what was
known \emph{then}. \code{get_series_vintages()} returns the whole as-known-then matrix
(every value of every observation as it stood in each real-time window) — the
honest surface for a point-in-time backtest. See \code{get_series_vintages()} for why
this matters.
